&lt;!-- https://www.pixelcut.ai/ai-image-editor?tool=removeBackground --&gt;</description></item><item><title>Humanoid Robot - Basic Motion Generation</title><link>https://fxrarz.github.io/projects/humanoid_robot_baic_motion/</link><pubDate>Mon, 25 Oct 2021 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://fxrarz.github.io/projects/humanoid_robot_baic_motion/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;A 17 Degree-of-Freedom (DoF) small humanoid robot without dexterous hand was controlled as a open-loop system. It has its own battery, motor driver and controller. The following action sequences was meticulously crafed to maintain balance and avoid fall.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="greetings"&gt;Greetings&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div style="position: relative; padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0; overflow: hidden;"&gt;
&lt;iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen" loading="eager" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/D9-itOW8qZg?autoplay=0&amp;amp;controls=1&amp;amp;end=0&amp;amp;loop=0&amp;amp;mute=0&amp;amp;start=0" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border:0;" title="YouTube video"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Basic greeting actions were performed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="football-kick"&gt;Football Kick&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div style="position: relative; padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0; overflow: hidden;"&gt;
&lt;iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen" loading="eager" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/8uqxAh4KmHA?autoplay=0&amp;amp;controls=1&amp;amp;end=0&amp;amp;loop=0&amp;amp;mute=0&amp;amp;start=0" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border:0;" title="YouTube video"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Standing on one leg while active balance is difficult for humans. Here the humanoid robot is made to stand in one leg and made to do ball kick action.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="walking"&gt;Walking&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div style="position: relative; padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0; overflow: hidden;"&gt;
&lt;iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen" loading="eager" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/g-9OWxfsHx4?autoplay=0&amp;amp;controls=1&amp;amp;end=0&amp;amp;loop=0&amp;amp;mute=0&amp;amp;start=0" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border:0;" title="YouTube video"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The primary challenge with legged humanoids lies in balance control. With only two legs, the support polygon reduces to a line. If the robot’s center of gravity (CG) moves beyond this support line, a fall becomes inevitable. Actively maintaining the CG within this boundary is therefore critical during walking, running, stair climbing, and even idle standing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- ## Motion Control using DL
&lt;div style="position: relative; padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0; overflow: hidden;"&gt;
&lt;iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen" loading="eager" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/UsmOGdB1qRQ?autoplay=0&amp;amp;controls=1&amp;amp;end=0&amp;amp;loop=0&amp;amp;mute=0&amp;amp;start=0" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border:0;" title="YouTube video"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
--&gt;
&lt;!-- ## Bipedal DRL in 2D sim --&gt;
&lt;h2 id="key-features"&gt;Key Features:&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mimic prerecorded actions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Untethred system&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On-board computation unit&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id="major-challenges"&gt;Major Challenges&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Control of open-loop systems&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;High computational demand for training and inference&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Actuator variations leading to gait instability or falls&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Complex and error-prone wire management&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Limited funding, time, and expertise&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
